      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Western Red Cedar, a beautiful choice for a deck build in Vancouver. The main difference between live load and dead load is quite straightforward, but it's a bit like trying to navigate a gypsy's market stall - you need to know where to look. \n\nLive load refers to the weight of people, furniture, and other items that will be on the deck, whereas dead load is the weight of the deck itself, including the materials and any permanent features like railings or stairs. Think of it like a goblin's treasure hoard: the live load is the weight of the goblin's precious cargo, while the dead load is the weight of the treasure chest itself.\n\nIn terms of sizing calculations, the live load is typically 40 psf (pounds per square foot), while the dead load is usually around 10-15 psf. To ensure proper post spacing, you'll want to use the dead load calculation to determine the required spacing, and then add a safety factor for the live load. It's like adding a little extra room for the goblin's mischief - you never know when they might decide to bring an extra friend or two to the party.\n\nFor example, if you're using a 4x6 post",
